To model the mission as a goal model, we structure the main goal into two phases: Collection and Delivery. Each phase is further decomposed into tasks, considering parallel execution and sequential steps where necessary.

### Goals and Tasks Breakdown

| Goal ID | Goal Title | Goal Text | Runtime Annotation | Goal Type | Relation |
|---------|------------|-----------|--------------------|-----------|-----------|
| G1      | Deliver Resources | Deliver required resources from storage to specified location | - | Achieve | - |
| G2      | Collection Phase | Collect resources from storage | Parallel | Perform | AND |
| G3      | Delivery Phase | Deliver collected resources | Parallel | Perform | AND |

### Tasks Breakdown

| Task ID | Task Title | Task Text | Relation | Location | Number of Robots |
|---------|------------|-----------|-----------|----------|--------------------|
| AT1     | Go to Storage | Navigate to storage location | AND | Storage | 1 |
| AT2     | Request Resources | Send request to storage | AND | Storage | 1 |
| AT3     | Retrieve Resources | Collect requested resources | AND | Storage | 1 |
| AT4     | Transport Resources | Move resources to destination | AND | Destination | 1 |
| AT5     | Monitor Battery | Check battery level | AND | Current Location | 1 |

### Explanation

- **G1 (Deliver Resources)**: The main goal is to deliver resources, decomposed into two phases (G2 and G3) connected in an AND relationship, meaning both must succeed.
  
- **G2 (Collection Phase)**: This phase involves three sequential tasks (AT1, AT2, AT3). The tasks are executed in parallel since multiple robots can collect from different storages simultaneously.

- **G3 (Delivery Phase)**: This phase includes two tasks (AT4 and AT5). AT4 transports the resources, while AT5 monitors the battery. Both tasks are executed in parallel, allowing the robot to monitor the battery while delivering.

This model ensures efficient resource delivery by leveraging parallel execution in both collection and delivery phases, while tasks handle specific actions and conditions.
